# lspbridge.service — systemd USER unit template for the lspbroker daemon
# (github.com/davidwalter0/lspbridge/cmd/lspbroker).
#
# Installed by `make install-service`, which runs a whole-file `sed` over
# this template to fill in the placeholders below (binary / socket path /
# spawn PATH — see the Makefile's LSPBROKER_EXEC / LSPBROKER_SOCKET /
# LSPBROKER_PATH variables) and writes the result to
# ~/.config/systemd/user/lspbridge.service (or an override dir when
# staging). NOTE: because the substitution is whole-file, this comment
# block deliberately avoids spelling out the placeholder tokens themselves
# — doing so here would let sed mangle this prose too. Look at the
# [Service] section below to see the tokens in place.
#
# This is a USER unit (not system-scope) by design: no root, no sudo. The
# daemon spawns per-project language servers (pyright, typescript-language-
# server, rust-analyzer, the Dart Analysis Server, ngserver) on behalf of
# whichever user is running an editor/agent session, and has no business
# running as anyone else. See docs/design/broker-lifecycle-and-socket-path.org
# for the full deployment story.
#
# WHY THE SOCKET PATH IS PASSED EXPLICITLY, NOT LEFT TO THE BARE DEFAULT
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# lspbroker's built-in default is $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/lspbridge/broker.sock when
# XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is set, else <TMPDIR-or-/tmp>/lspbridge-<uid>/
# (pkg/broker.DefaultSocketPath, which falls through to os.TempDir() —
# itself $TMPDIR-or-"/tmp" — in the empty-XDG_RUNTIME_DIR branch). A
# systemd --user unit ALWAYS has XDG_RUNTIME_DIR set (systemd exports it to
# every unit it spawns), so left to its own default THIS DAEMON would
# always bind under $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/lspbridge/ — but whether that is also
# where the CONSUMERS (mcp-ast, mcp-agent-editor) will dial depends entirely
# on what environment the mgk MCP gateway hands them when it spawns them,
# which is a separate, independently-changing fact this unit cannot assume.
#
# RE-MEASURED 2026-09-25 (supersedes the note below, kept as history): both
# consumers now carry XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/<uid> in their real spawn
# environment (verified live against /proc/<pid>/environ for both), so the
# consumer's resolution and this daemon's bare default now COINCIDE. The
# socket is still pinned explicitly rather than left bare, for two reasons
# that hold independently of whether they currently agree: (1) the exact
# path a reader should expect to see live is stated right here rather than
# requiring a trip into pkg/broker to work it out, and (2) `make doctor`
# (cmd/lspdoctor's consumerSocketPath) independently re-derives what the
# CONSUMER resolves on every run and will flag a [FAIL] socket-path
# divergence the moment this pin and that value disagree again — which can
# only happen by a change in this file's LSPBROKER_SOCKET value or in the
# consumer's real spawn environment, not silently. The pin is
# `%t/lspbridge/broker.sock` — see the [Service] section: `%t` is a systemd
# specifier ("$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR ... for user managers", systemd.unit(5)),
# expanded by systemd itself at unit load, so it can never disagree with
# the user manager's own runtime directory and needs no `id -u` shell
# arithmetic on the Makefile side.
#
# IF THE CONSUMER'S ENVIRONMENT DIVERGES FROM XDG_RUNTIME_DIR AGAIN: `%t`
# stops being correct here (it tracks THIS daemon's XDG_RUNTIME_DIR, not the
# consumer's actual resolution), and LSPBROKER_SOCKET must go back to naming
# the consumer's real path explicitly — the same fix applied in 2026-08-12,
# below. Re-verify against /proc/<consumer-pid>/environ before assuming the
# fix in either direction still matches reality; see
# docs/design/broker-lifecycle-and-socket-path.org and 4.11 in the fleet
# rules ("recorded claims are timestamped hypotheses") for why.
#
# SUPERSEDED 2026-08-12 REASONING (history, not current — kept rather than
# deleted): at that time, both consumers were spawned by the mgk MCP gateway
# under a FILTERED environment with NEITHER XDG_RUNTIME_DIR NOR TMPDIR set
# (verified live against /proc/<mcp-ast-pid>/environ), so they fell through
# both branches of DefaultSocketPath and landed on the literal "/tmp"
# default — a path this daemon was never listening on if left to its own
# bare default (which would have bound under /run/user/<uid>/, since
# systemd always sets XDG_RUNTIME_DIR for units it spawns). That was a
# silent mismatch, not a crash: every semantic query failed as "broker not
# running" even though it was up. The socket was therefore pinned to a
# LITERAL /tmp path at that time, and the Makefile's LSPBROKER_SOCKET
# comment records the specific $TMPDIR-arithmetic bug found while building
# that fix. Neither the literal path nor that reasoning is current — see
# above — but both are kept here as the shape of a divergence this unit
# must be able to recognize and re-fix if it recurs.
#
# WHY A PLAIN ALWAYS-ON SERVICE, NOT SOCKET ACTIVATION
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# lspbroker has no systemd socket-activation support today — nothing in
# cmd/lspbroker reads LISTEN_FDS or accepts an inherited listener. An
# Accept=no [Socket] unit would hand it descriptors it simply ignores, and
# it would fall back to binding the path itself anyway. Separately, the
# daemon's own -idle-timeout reaps idle language-server SESSIONS, not the
# broker process itself — the broker is meant to be long-lived by design.
# Socket activation is a real option for later (tracked in the design doc)
# but needs an ExecStart-adjacent change to cmd/lspbroker/main.go first;
# this unit does not assume it.
#
# WHY THE SPAWN PATH IS SET EXPLICITLY
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# This daemon's entire job is to exec.LookPath and spawn language servers.
# A systemd --user unit's default PATH is a minimal system one (no
# ~/.local/bin, no ~/.cargo/bin — measured 2026-08-10), so left unset the
# unit would start cleanly, report active, and then fail every single
# query with a missing-binary error: healthy-looking and non-functional at
# once. The value below is captured from the shell that ran
# `make install-service`, not invented — re-run install-service from a
# shell that can already find these tools if you add or move one.
[Unit]
Description=lspbroker — shared LSP session broker (lspbridge)
After=network.target

[Service]
Environment=PATH=__PATH__
ExecStart=__EXEC__ -socket __SOCKET__
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=3

[Install]
WantedBy=default.target
